::ALG2_U04_L03_GP_Q01::
Question ID: ALG2_U04_L03_GP_Q01
MLA Standard: MLA.A2.FM.2; MLA.A2.FM.3; MLA.A2.FM.4
Question:
Which model represents 900 dollars earning 4% annual interest?
{
=A. A(t)=900(1.04)ᵗ#Correct. The principal is 900 and 4% growth gives factor 1.04.
~B. A(t)=900(0.96)ᵗ#This models 4% depreciation, not interest growth.
~C. A(t)=1.04(900)ᵗ#This reverses the principal and factor.
~D. A(t)=900+4t#This adds 4 dollars per year instead of 4%.
}

::ALG2_U04_L03_GP_Q02::
Question ID: ALG2_U04_L03_GP_Q02
MLA Standard: MLA.A2.FM.2; MLA.A2.FM.3; MLA.A2.FM.4
Question:
A 15000 dollar car depreciates 10% per year. Which factor should be used?
{
~A. 1.10#This models 10% growth.
=B. 0.90#Correct. If 10% is lost, 90% remains, so the factor is 0.90.
~C. 0.10#This is the rate lost, not the remaining factor.
~D. 1.90#This does not represent 10% depreciation.
}

::ALG2_U04_L03_GP_Q03::
Question ID: ALG2_U04_L03_GP_Q03
MLA Standard: MLA.A2.FM.2; MLA.A2.FM.3; MLA.A2.FM.4
Question:
In A(t)=2000(1.06)ᵗ, what does 2000 represent?
{
~A. The interest rate#The rate is 6%, represented by the factor 1.06.
~B. The number of years#The variable t represents time.
=C. The initial principal#Correct. The coefficient gives the starting amount at t = 0.
~D. The final balance#The final balance depends on t.
}

::ALG2_U04_L03_GP_Q04::
Question ID: ALG2_U04_L03_GP_Q04
MLA Standard: MLA.A2.FM.2; MLA.A2.FM.3; MLA.A2.FM.4
Question:
Which model represents a 25000 dollar asset losing 18% each year?
{
~A. V(t)=25000(1.18)ᵗ#This models 18% growth.
~B. V(t)=0.82(25000)ᵗ#This reverses the initial value and factor.
~C. V(t)=25000-18t#This subtracts 18 units per year, not 18%.
=D. V(t)=25000(0.82)ᵗ#Correct. Losing 18% means 82% remains each year.
}

::ALG2_U04_L03_GP_Q05::
Question ID: ALG2_U04_L03_GP_Q05
MLA Standard: MLA.A2.FM.2; MLA.A2.FM.3; MLA.A2.FM.4
Question:
Two accounts have models A(t)=1000(1.04)ᵗ and B(t)=900(1.07)ᵗ. What should be true for a fair comparison?
{
=A. Use the same value of t for both accounts.#Correct. Financial choices should be compared over the same time interval.
~B. Use different years for each account.#Different time intervals make the comparison unfair.
~C. Ignore the initial values.#Initial values affect account balances.
~D. Compare only the growth rates.#The rate matters, but starting values also matter.
}

